Title: GOOD MOURNING
Credit: Episode 106
Author: Development draft
Draft date: 6 November 2026
Source: Please Stay on the Line
Notes: First draft. Target 9:45 story plus 0:15 credits. Leave room for listening and ordinary tasks; do not treat the ending as a cure.

# 106.1 | 00:00-01:15

INT. COOPERATIVE CLINIC DESK - DAY

SUPER: TWO WEEKS LATER.

The canteen has acquired a private consultation room and a worse filing cabinet. It has not become a beautiful new institution overnight.

Mara, in her own clothes, sits behind an administration desk. A small sign identifies her job. Not director. Not clinician.

Mrs. Dev pushes an appointment card toward her.

MRS. DEV
I had Tuesdays at nine.

MARA
Nadi's here Tuesdays at ten. The bus—

MRS. DEV
I know about the bus. I wanted my old time.

Mara checks the schedule. No inspirational slogan intervenes.

MARA
I can't give you nine. Ten-fifteen leaves time from the stop. Or I can ask the other provider about nine next week.

Dev considers two actual options.

MRS. DEV
Ten-fifteen. This week.

Mara writes it down. Separately confirms Dev's continued treatment choice.

MRS. DEV
You're not going to call me brave?

MARA
Not on the appointment card.

Dev almost smiles. Takes the card.

Beyond the desk, Nadi turns three choice cards toward another resident. The conversation stays private.

# 106.2 | 01:15-02:45

INT. CANTEEN ACCOUNTS CORNER - CONTINUOUS

Tessa shows Suri a statement. Mara listens from the desk while sorting blank forms.

TESSA
Seven days of reserve bridge. Three thousand five hundred and seventy spent. The remaining one thousand five hundred and thirty never left. Five days reimbursed so far. Two pending reconciliation.

SURI
And the escrow?

TESSA
Still sealed. It's not in that sum.

She places the clinic payroll receipt beside a different line: ORDERED TRANSITION ALLOCATION.

TESSA
These hours are paid from future administration charges under the order. Review next Friday. Not a permanent blank cheque.

Suri makes a note beside the north-window repairs.

LEN enters with his FATHER, late 70s, who chooses a chair by the window. Len brings a renewal card to the desk.

LEN
He wants to continue. And I can't cover late shift.

MARA
You're not on it.

Len checks. Believes her only after looking.

A notice on Mara's screen: DIRECTOR PELL SUSPENDED PENDING INVESTIGATION. INTERIM SUPERVISOR APPOINTED.

Isha, bringing clean cups, sees it.

ISHA
Did he send a statement?

MARA
Yes.

ISHA
Are we supposed to answer?

MARA
No.

Len takes his father's coat as Nadi calls him in. He waits to help him out of it, not hurrying him toward the appointment.

# 106.3 | 02:45-04:15

INT. PRIVATE CONSULTATION ROOM - AFTERNOON

Nadi closes the door. Mara is in the patient chair. Her staff forms stay outside.

NADI
You chose to defer at the annex. Still what you want?

MARA
For now. I don't want that to become a promise I can't change.

NADI
It isn't.

MARA
I slept four hours. Then two more. I made lunch and forgot I'd made it. I ate it cold.

Nadi notes the facts.

NADI
Are you able to work the shorter shifts we agreed?

MARA
Yes. The quiet parts are harder.

NADI
Who can you call when they're hard?

MARA
Isha. You. I haven't asked anyone else.

NADI
Let's make the list bigger than one sister.

Mara nods. Nadi offers a support-group time without filling in Mara's name for her.

MARA
Sometimes it's nothing. I think I'm doing it wrong.

NADI
There's no attendance requirement for a feeling.

Mara laughs unexpectedly. Wipes her eye, annoyed by the timing.

NADI
We can review the plan. Including treatment if you want it. Friday?

Mara checks her own calendar.

MARA
Friday.

She books it herself.

# 106.4 | 04:15-05:30

INT. COOPERATIVE CLINIC DESK - LATE AFTERNOON

Martin places his phone on the desk. The same phone as in the first episode.

MARTIN
Seven's no better. I'm on the bus then.

Mara opens the message-service settings. Her restricted screen allows account requests, not clinical chart access.

MARA
You want a different time?

MARTIN
I want them to stop.

She pauses. The old answer almost arrives.

MARA
All messages?

MARTIN
All messages. Keep the clinic.

Two separate requests. She completes them separately.

MARA
Messages canceled. Clinical appointment unchanged. That's the confirmation for both.

He reads the receipt.

MARTIN
I remember her voice. Some days I don't want to hear it on a bus.

MARA
You don't have to explain.

He picks up the phone. Waits for the usual closing line.

Mara lets the exchange be finished.

MARTIN
Right.

He leaves. Not cured. Not ungrateful. Done with this particular transaction.

Mara closes the account screen before the next person sits down.

# 106.5 | 05:30-07:00

INT. VENN APARTMENT KITCHEN - EVENING

A small room with mismatched chairs. The ugly kettle is home from the canteen. Isha carries the recorder from a shelf to the table.

ISHA
We can stop it.

MARA
I know.

ISHA
I'm saying it anyway.

Mara puts both feet on the floor.

MARA
Play it.

The recorder clicks. Real tape-machine hiss, or its battered digital equivalent. A button pressed twice by someone irritated with buttons.

RENA (V.O.)
Is this on? Don't buy those pears again. They're potatoes with ambition.

The same cough heard earlier. Mara hears the whole of it this time. Isha watches the recorder, not Mara's performance.

RENA (V.O.)
And stop asking if I'm comfortable. I'm not. Come and sit down. Either of you. Both, if you're going to be civil.

A distant television on Rena's recording. She shifts something against the microphone.

RENA (V.O.)
I was sharp with you this morning. I'm tired of being looked after. That isn't the same as being tired of you.

Mara puts her hand flat on the table. Does not press STOP.

RENA (V.O.)
Milk. We do need milk. And not those little cartons. I don't want my last contribution to this house to be a cupboard full of milk thimbles.

A small, unplanned laugh from Isha.

RENA (V.O.)
Bring the blue mug. It's too hot in the thin one. That's all. Come and sit.

A fumbling click. The recording ends. No hidden second message.

# 106.6 | 07:00-08:30

INT. VENN APARTMENT KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

The sisters listen to the recorder's silence.

Isha is still smiling at milk thimbles. Mara begins to cry. Quietly, without a cinematic first-tear announcement.

MARA
I'm sorry I made every meal an appointment.

ISHA
You missed a few appointments.

Mara laughs through the tears. It makes nothing tidier.

MARA
I thought if you were upset and I didn't fix it, I'd be leaving you with it.

ISHA
You can be in the room while I'm upset.

Mara nods.

ISHA
I still get angry with you.

MARA
I know.

ISHA
Don't turn that into another project.

A longer silence. Mara stays seated.

Isha fills the kettle. The switch falls back up.

Mara reaches for the base. Stops herself from taking the job over.

MARA
Hold it or cups?

ISHA
Hold it. I've been holding it for years.

Mara holds the switch. Isha gets cups.

Steam develops slowly. Mara's tears come and go while water heats. Isha doesn't change her tone to mark them.

Isha switches the recorder fully off. It won't begin again by itself.

# 106.7 | 08:30-09:45

INT. COOPERATIVE CORRIDOR - SAME EVENING

Suri checks the wall schedule: QUIET HOUR — RESIDENT VOTE, 19:00 TO 20:00. REVIEW IN FOURTEEN DAYS.

The building speaker wakes.

BUILDING VOICE
We hope you are having—

Suri turns the scheduled-announcement switch to QUIET. Signs the log beside the time. A simple duty, not a coronation.

INT. VENN APARTMENT KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

Outside: traffic, a child protesting bedtime, a door shutting too hard. The world has not been muted for these two women.

Isha sets down three cups. One blue, two ordinary. The blue one stays empty.

Mara's hand moves toward it, old tidying reflex.

She leaves it where it is.

The kettle boils. Mara releases the switch. Isha pours into the other two cups.

They sit at the same table, no tablet between them.

ISHA
Tea's too hot.

MARA
We can wait.

They do.

Stay with the room. Not a vision of Rena in the empty chair. Not an answering voice. Just the place they have made for what isn't there.

CUT TO BLACK.

[[09:45-10:00 — End credits over ordinary city sound. No reassuring service chime.]]
